END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Oak Discovery Day
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261017T100000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261017T153000
UID:261014HjufrNd17E6Q3IBW-6fImQ
DESCRIPTION:Visit the Hopland Research and Extension Center for a day of l
 earning and celebration of our local oak woodland ecosystem. Talk to oak e
 xperts\, sign up for a guided walk\, or make an oak inspired craft! This f
 amily friendly event will give visitors opportunities to create and explor
 e\, while learning more about California's iconic oaks. \n\nOn arrival at
  the Rod Shippey Hall you will be greeted by an art exhibit of photographs
  and paintings inspired by our local oaks and shared by the community. A v
 ariety of activities and tables will be spread between the woodlands on th
 e patio focused on community science projects\, oak woodland management an
 d regeneration\, acorn processing\, and oak identification. For the kids a
 nd everyone looking to unleash their creativity\, there will be a large ar
 t station featuring sun printing\, stamping\, painting\, and more.\n\nFor 
 those wanting to see more of the site\, a variety of walks and tours will 
 be held: \n\n10am-2pm: Oak arts and information booths.\n\n10:30am-12:00p
 m: Oak Woodland guided walk with naturalist and author Kate Marianchild\n\
 n10:30am-12:00pm: A Beginners Birding Walk guided by Helen Magruder Menasi
 an\n\n12:30-2:00pm: Nature Journaling with artist and ecologist Robin Lee 
 Carlson\n\n12:30-2:00 pm: Amazing Oaks of Hopland REC with botanist Jen Ri
 ddell and UCCE Oak Specialist\, Lina Aoyama.\n\n2:30-3:30pm: A performance
  of And Who Shall Heal the Ground\, an original play organized by Quercus
  Collaborative member Diana Pepetone.\n\nSliding scale admission from $0-$
 10 per person.\n\nSnacks will be available\, but please bring your own pac
 ked lunch to enjoy at the picnic area.\n\nThis event is made possible than
 ks to the support of the Quercus Symbiosis Foundation\, and the volunteer 
 group The Quercus Collaborative.
LOCATION:University of California Hopland Research and Extension Center
URL:https://link.ucanr.edu/oakday26
X-SOURCE:Press Democrat
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:The Green Machine w/ Tumbleweed Soul Duo
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261017T193000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261017T223000
UID:261014C_OG7CNLDEyJN7Hx01V01w
DESCRIPTION:$15 General Admission\n\nDoors at 7:30pm.\n\nShow from 8:15-10
 :30pm with one intermission\n\nEarlybirds!\n\n$3 off each ticket when you 
 use the code earlybird15 at checkout. Offer ends October 11th.\n\nPromo
  Codes are not applicable at the door.\n\nCodes are not case sensitive\,
  but must be typed as one word.\n\nThe Green Machine is a Petaluma-based t
 rio playing what we call “Garage Americana.” We are rooted in classic 
 country and 60s rock\; inspired by songwriters like Merle Haggard and Gram
  Parsons and groups like the Stones\, Creedence and the Byrds. Adam Walsh 
 and Stephanie Salva\, Tumbleweed Soul Duo\, are best known for their joint
  role in the beloved Sonoma County band the Jug Dealers. With powerful har
 monies and eclectic songwriting that is both familiar yet fresh\, their gr
 owing catalogue of original songs reflects their love of the deep city juk
 e joints and country shacks where Americana was born. www.thegreenmachineb
 and.com Tumbleweed Soul is an American Roots project based in the San Fran
 cisco North Bay\, led by Adam Walsh and Stephanie Salva. The pair have bee
 n rocking the West Coast music scene in various projects for over a decade
 \, best known for their joint role in the beloved Sonoma County band the J
 ug Dealers.  Joining them is Mike Dipirro on bass \\(Free Peoples\, The T
 richromes\\)\, and bay area legend\, Greg Anton on drums \\(Zero\, The Roc
 k Collection\\). With powerful harmonies and eclectic songwriting that is 
 both familiar yet fresh\, their growing catalogue of original songs reflec
 ts their love of the deep city juke joints and country shacks where Americ
 ana was born.  Jazz\, Blues\, Soul\, R&B\, Country\, Funk\, and Old Timey
  Traditionals all find a home at a Tumbleweed show. tumbleweedsoul.com
LOCATION:The Lost Church
URL:https://thelostchurch.my.salesforce-sites.com/ticket#/instances/a0FUh0
 0000CbqlVMAR
X-SOURCE:Press Democrat
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:4th Annual NAMIWalks Sonoma County
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261017T100000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261017T133000
UID:261013mUQzyhK-h0qyZImk9Pbing
DESCRIPTION:NAMI Sonoma County holds its 4th annual NAMIWalk event at Howa
 rth Park on Saturday\, October 17\, 2026. This free community event\, is o
 pen to all\, and dedicated to raising community mental health awareness an
 d advocating for access to behavioral health resources for all in our comm
 unity. Enjoy live music\, 25 community mental health organizations who wil
 l share their resources\, support dogs to cuddle\, kids' activities\, grea
 t raffle prizes\, and a short speaking program at 11:00 am. Join us to sho
 w your support for a community where mental health matters and help us rai
 se funds to keep our education programs\, support groups\, presentations\,
  trainings\, and Warmline\, free of cost for all.\n\n**Register at www.nam
 iwalks.org/sonomacounty.**
LOCATION:Howarth Park\, Cypress Point Picnic Area
URL:https://namiwalks.org/sonomacounty
X-SOURCE:Press Democrat
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Cotati Fall Festival at the Veronda-Falletti Ranch
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261017T100000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261017T160000
UID:261013SmiQigHklUe1iByGrfJ14A
DESCRIPTION:Come on down for our 4th Annual Cotati Fall Festival!\n\nFesti
 val Details -\n\nDate: Oct 17\n\nTimes: 10:00 am - 4:00 pm\n\nSensory Frie
 ndly Hour - 10:00am - 11:00am\n\nGeneral Admission - 11:00am-4:00pm\n\nLoc
 ation: Veronda-Falletti Ranch\, 175 West Sierra Avenue\, Cotati\n\nFee: $6
  per person over 2 - pre-registration\, $8 at the gate\n\nRegistration enc
 ouraged and will save a lot of time at the gate but drop ins are welcome.
  \n\nAll ages welcome!\n\nChildren 2 and under are free and do not need t
 heir own registration.\n\nRegister online at: https://cotati.recdesk.com/C
 ommunity/Program/Detail?programId=1290\n\nLive music & entertainment by "F
 armer" Arann Harris (11am-12pm) and Hannah Jern-Miller (1pm to 4pm)\n\nAll
 -Day Stations include:\n\nPumpkin patch\n\nPumpkin painting\n\nHay bale py
 ramid\n\nSensory bins\n\nGames & crafts\n\nFarm stand - farm grown flower 
 bouquets\, dried flowers\, fresh produce\, pumpkins\n\nVisits withe the an
 imals\n\nConcession Stand\n\nAnd more!\n\nCome visit our pumpkin patch. Ch
 ildren get to pick a pumpkin to decorate and take home\, participate in ga
 mes and crafts\, enjoy the live music and entertainment\, visit the animal
 s and enjoy some yummy treats. Many photo opportunities will be available.
 \n\nWe recommend closed toe shoes.\n\n\\*Sensory Friendly hour will be fro
 m 10am-11am. This hour will have limited space available\, no loud or live
  music\, and lots of opportunities for sensory play.\n\nTickets to Sensory
  Friendly Hour: https://cotati.recdesk.com/Community/Program/Detail?progr
 amId=1438\n\nIf have questions\, please feel free to reach out to us via e
 mail at awilson@cotaticity.gov or 707-665-4222.
LOCATION:Veronda-Falletti Ranch
URL:https://cotati.recdesk.com/Community/Program/Detail?programId=1290
X-SOURCE:Press Democrat
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Using AI to Bring Your Presentation to Life
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261017T123000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261017T140000
UID:261013975t1BjzFEqw2VQ10PJ0sA
DESCRIPTION:The Sonoma County Genealogical Society Presents\n\n **Using AI
  to Bring Your Presentation to Life**\n\n **Presented by Jane Pritchard**\
 n\n Jane will present the slide show she created for the Santa Rosa Chapte
 r of the DAR honoring her Revolutionary War patriot\, Margaret Eskridge Ke
 nner\, who supported Revolutionary troops with food and supplies. Along th
 e way she taught herself to use AI assistants Claude and Nano Banana\, to 
 speed up research and to build both the PowerPoint and a companion PDF. Sh
 e will walk through the simple\, practical ways she used AI — and where 
 it fell short so members can try the same approach with their own ancestor
 s.\n\nJane began serious family research in 2021\, during the pandemic\, a
 nd has since taken many genealogy classes through Santa Rosa Junior Colleg
 e. She was surprised to discover that her ancestors played a part in the f
 ounding of this country — a history her family had never known. That res
 earch led to her recent membership in the DAR\, where she now serves on th
 e chapter's Lineage Research Committee helping prospective members documen
 t their lines. A native of West Virginia\, Jane has lived in Santa Rosa si
 nce 1981. She is a mother and grandmother and has practiced as a Jin Shin 
 Jyutsu acupressure therapist for 36 years.\n\n Saturday\, October 17th\n\
 n Meeting starts at 12:30 pm\, presentation starts at 1:00 pm PST\n\nMeet
 ing Details : The meeting is held via Zoom. Login begins at 12:30 p.m.\n\n
  FREE Via ZOOM\n\n Register at **https://www.scgsonline.org**\n\n Regis
 tration closes Friday\,  October 16th
URL:https://www.scgsonline.org
X-SOURCE:Press Democrat
END:VEVENT
